<span class="kicker">2026-08-28 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>Is Silica Sand Recommended for Stone Paver Walkways?</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Silica sand is a common, traditional choice for filling joints between stone pavers, and it does the job reasonably well in the right situation. Whether it’s actually the best choice for your specific walkway depends on how it stacks up against polymeric sand, which has become the more widely recommended option for most modern paver installations, stone included. </p>
<p>The short answer is that silica sand isn’t a bad choice, but for most stone walkways, polymeric sand offers real advantages that are worth understanding before you decide.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we get this question fairly often from homeowners planning a natural stone walkway across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and the sand choice matters more than people often expect. This guide compares silica sand and polymeric sand directly, covers safety considerations during installation, and addresses how irregular stone joints, like those found in flagstone, change the calculation.</p>
<h2><b>Silica Sand Versus Polymeric Sand at a Glance</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Silica Sand</b></td><td><b>Polymeric Sand</b></td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>Natural, uncoated sand</td><td>Sand with added polymer binders</td></tr><tr><td>Weed resistance</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Erosion resistance</td><td>Low, washes out over time</td><td>High, resists washout once cured</td></tr><tr><td>Ease of installation</td><td>Simple, no activation needed</td><td>Requires misting to activate binders</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Very tight, uniform joints; budget projects</td><td>Most walkways, especially wider or irregular joints</td></tr><tr><td>Reapplication frequency</td><td>Every 1 to 2 years typically</td><td>Every 3 to 5 years typically</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The core difference comes down to the polymer additives in polymeric sand, which bind the sand particles together once activated with water, creating a semi-rigid joint fill that resists washing out, resists weed growth, and holds up notably better over time than plain silica sand.</p>
<h2><b>What Silica Sand Actually Is</b></h2>
<p>Silica sand is simply fine, uncoated sand, often the same base material used in polymeric sand before the polymer binders are added. It’s been used for decades as a straightforward, inexpensive joint fill for paver and stone installations, and it remains widely available and easy to work with.</p>
<h2><b>Advantages of Silica Sand</b></h2>
<h3><b>Lower Cost</b></h3>
<p>Silica sand is generally less expensive than polymeric sand, both per bag and in labor time, since it doesn’t require the misting and activation step polymeric sand needs.</p>
<h3><b>Simple Application</b></h3>
<p>Sweeping silica sand into joints and compacting is a straightforward process without the timing considerations polymeric sand requires, misting at the right stage, avoiding overwatering, and working within a reasonable window before the polymers begin to set.</p>
<h3><b>Works Reasonably Well for Very Tight, Uniform Joints</b></h3>
<p>On a stone walkway with consistently narrow, uniform joints, closer to an eighth of an inch than a quarter inch or more, silica sand can perform adequately, since there’s less open joint space for water and debris to work into and wash it out over time.</p>
<h2><b>Disadvantages of Silica Sand</b></h2>
<h3><b>Prone to Washing Out</b></h3>
<p>Without any binding agent, silica sand shifts and washes out of joints over time, particularly after heavy rain or repeated watering from nearby plantings. This is the single biggest practical drawback compared to polymeric sand, and it means more frequent maintenance to keep joints properly filled.</p>
<h3><b>Doesn’t Resist Weed Growth</b></h3>
<p>Loose silica sand offers little resistance to weed seeds settling into joints and establishing roots, which becomes an ongoing maintenance issue, particularly for walkways in sunny areas or near existing garden beds where seed sources are nearby.</p>
<h3><b>More Frequent Reapplication</b></h3>
<p>Because it washes out and settles more readily than polymeric sand, silica sand joints typically need topping off every one to two years, compared to the three to five year interval more common with a properly installed polymeric product.</p>

